      ],
      "text": "25–26 Which TWO recommendations for getting out of a cycle of\nprocrastination does the writer give?\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        136.71470642089844,
        252.94720458984375,
        155.51361083984375
      ],
      "text": "A. not judging ourselves harshly\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        75.12000274658203,
        156.63470458984375,
        400.1717529296875,
        234.95361328125
      ],
      "text": "B. setting ourselves manageable aims\nC. rewarding ourselves for tasks achieved\nD. prioritising tasks according to their importance\nE. avoiding things that stop us concentrating on our tasks\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        250.7720489501953,
        192.74929809570312,
        270.2734375
      ],
      "text": "READING PASSAGE 3\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        285.571044921875,
        249.30880737304688,
        305.07244873046875
      ],
      "text": "Invasion of the Robot Umpires\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        319.7146911621094,
        506.542236328125,
        457.6736145019531
      ],
      "text": "A few years ago, Fred DeJesus from Brooklyn, New York became the first\numpire in a minor league baseball game to use something called the\nAutomated Ball-Strike System (ABS), often referred to as the 'robo-umpire'.\nInstead of making any judgments himself about a strike*, DeJesus had\ndecisions fed to him through an earpiece, connected to a modified missile-\ntracking system. The contraption looked like a large black pizza box with one\nglowing green eye; it was mounted above the press stand.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        470.793701171875,
        526.7379150390625,
        648.4736328125
      ],
      "text": "Major League Baseball (MLB), who had commissioned the system, wanted\nhuman umpires to announce the calls, just as they would have done in the past.\nWhen the first pitch came in, a recorded voice told DeJesus it was a strike.\nPreviously, calling a strike was a judgment call on the part of the umpire. Even\nif the batter does not hit the ball, a pitch that passes through the 'strike zone'\n(an imaginary zone about seventeen inches wide, stretching from the batter's\nknees to the middle of his chest) is considered a strike. During that first game,\nwhen DeJesus announced calls, there was no heckling and no shouted\ndisagreement. Nobody said a word.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        661.5936889648438,
        505.81988525390625,
        759.8336181640625
      ],
      "text": "For a hundred and fifty years or so, the strike zone has been the game's\nanimating force – countless arguments between a team's manager and the\numpire have taken place over its boundaries and whether a ball had crossed\nthrough it. The rules of play have evolved in various stages. Today, everyone\nknows that you may scream your disagreement in an umpire's face, but you\n"
    }
  ]
